Designing Scalable E-commerce on AWS EKS - Part 2

Database Architecture & Auto-Scaling for MERN Stack

CLIENT: "How do we connect EKS to MongoDB Atlas securely?"
ARCHITECT: "VPC Peering - the standard approach:
Setup: Atlas creates peering request → Accept in AWS Console → Update route tables → Whitelist VPC CIDR
Connection: EKS Pods → VPC Peering → MongoDB Atlas (Private, no internet, free data transfer)"

CLIENT: "What if MongoDB Atlas goes down?"
ARCHITECT: "Multi-region cluster with automatic failover:
Primary: us-east-1 (Read/Write)
Secondary: us-west-2, eu-west-1 (Read replicas)
Failover in <10 seconds. Read from secondary for catalogs, primary for orders/payments."

CLIENT: "How does auto-scaling work?"
ARCHITECT: "Three-layer approach:
HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler):
Min: 3 pods, Max: 50 pods
Trigger: 70% CPU → Scales to 5 pods in 60 seconds
Scale down slowly after 5-min wait (prevents thrashing)
Cluster Autoscaler:
HPA requests 20 pods → Only 15 fit → 5 Pending
Launches 2 new nodes in <2 minutes
Mix 50% On-Demand + 50% Spot (70% cheaper)
Pod Disruption Budget:
Minimum 2 pods always running
Prevents updates from killing all pods"

CLIENT: "Walk me through Black Friday scaling."
ARCHITECT: "Real timeline:
11:59 PM: 100 req/sec, 3 pods
12:00 AM: Traffic spikes to 10K req/sec
30s: HPA scales to 15 pods
2min: Cluster adds nodes
5min: 50 pods, 18 nodes
3:00 AM: Traffic drops
Gradual scale down
Final: 10 pods, 4 nodes
30-second scaling vs 5-10 minutes with EC2!"

CLIENT: "How do health checks work?"
ARCHITECT: "Two probe types:
Liveness Probe:
Checks /health every 10s
Fails 3 times → Pod restarted
Detects: Deadlocks, hung processes
Readiness Probe:
Checks /ready every 5s
Validates MongoDB, Redis connections
Fails 2 times → Remove from traffic (pod keeps running)
When fixed → Auto-added back
Example: MongoDB drops temporarily → Pod removed from traffic → Reconnects → Pod added back (no restart needed!)"

CLIENT: "How ensure high availability?"
ARCHITECT: "Multi-AZ with anti-affinity:
Nodes span 3 AZs (us-east-1a/b/c)
No two pods on same node
Node failure: 1 pod affected
AZ failure: 33% affected
Combined with PDB: Service stays up"

CLIENT: "What about resource requests?"
ARCHITECT: "Based on load testing:
Backend: 200m CPU, 256Mi memory (handles 200 req/sec) Frontend: 100m CPU, 128Mi memory
Monitor with kubectl top pods, adjust based on real usage."

✅ KEY TAKEAWAYS
VPC Peering: Secure MongoDB connection
Multi-region: <10s automatic failover
HPA + CA: 30-second scaling
Liveness vs Readiness: Restart vs traffic removal
Multi-AZ: True high availability
Load testing: Right-sized resources
